Today I’m continuing my short series “Notes from an Associate Editor”, sharing reflections on how scientific publishing actually works, at least from my own experience.


Why do reviewers sometimes take time?

From the outside, peer review can look simple: a manuscript is submitted, reviewers are invited, and comments should arrive quickly. Right ? But in practice, it is rarely that straightforward.

One important reason is very simple: peer review for journals is largely voluntary work. It relies on researchers contributing time and expertise in addition to their own deadlines, teaching, supervision, grants, and administrative responsibilities. Inevitably, this means that reviewing is not at the top of the priority list for most people. Another uncomfortable truth is that a large proportion of researchers do not engage regularly in peer review at all, even though the system depends on this collective contribution to function.

Good peer review takes time because a careful evaluation is much more than reading a manuscript once. Reviewers usually need to examine the work in detail. They check whether the methods are clear, whether the conclusions are actually supported by the data, whether the relevant literature has been considered, and whether there are weaknesses or concerns that should be addressed before publication.

For this reason, a delayed review is not always a sign of lack of interest. In many cases, the reviewer accepts with the best intentions, but only begins to fully engage with the manuscript after downloading and reading it carefully. And sometimes the process becomes even more demanding because the manuscript is not clearly written, which increases the effort required to understand and evaluate it properly. This is especially true in highly specialized topics, where finding the right reviewer is already difficult in itself.

From the editorial side, this is one of the least visible parts of the process. Much of the work involves inviting reviewers, waiting for responses, sending reminders, deciding whether it is still reasonable to wait or whether it is time to move on, and trying to keep the process fair without creating unnecessary delays for authors.
This can be frustrating for authors, and understandably so. But it is often frustrating on the editorial side as well, especially when reviews are delayed for a long time without any response. After waiting for an extended period, editors are sometimes forced to look for a replacement reviewer simply because one of the invited reviewers is no longer engaging with the process.

Still, speed is not the only goal. What we really want is thoughtful, constructive, and scientifically meaningful review, something that genuinely helps improve the manuscript, supports the authors, and contributes to the progress of science more broadly.

Editorial delays are not always a sign of editorial inertia, and authors need to recognize that a fair review process depends on finding proactive reviewers who are willing to engage seriously and contribute meaningful scientific evaluation. It is particularly discouraging when a review takes many days and returns only a few superficial lines, or worse, comments that appear to have been generated by AI, because this signals a failure in the reviewer’s role and leaves the editor with the responsibility of finding better reviewers to protect the fairness and scientific quality of a process whose invisible contributions remain essential to publishing itself.

What do you think is the best way to make peer review both rigorous and more efficient?

